{
 "id": "22-04-pwm-brightness",
 "title": "Dim the night light with PWM",
 "kind": "transient",
 "story": "With a fresh 4.5 V battery, the LED draws about 19 mA whenever the transistor is on. The night light should give an average of about 5.7 mA (30% of full brightness). Choose the PWM signal for the microcontroller pin (slot S1, 1 kHz, 0 to 3.3 V). To measure the average current, a 1 ohm resistor sits under the transistor's emitter (so 1 mV means 1 mA) and a 10 kohm / 1 uF low-pass filter (time constant 10 ms) smooths its voltage, just like a multimeter would. After 60 ms the filtered voltage must read between 5.0 and 6.5 mV.",
